---
import core from '../lib/get-ready-package.json';
import mcp from '../lib/get-ready-mcp-package.json';
interface Props{kind?:'core'|'mcp'}
const isMcp=Astro.props.kind==='mcp',distribution=isMcp?mcp:core;
const filename=isMcp?'get-ready-mcp':'get-ready-core',titleId=isMcp?'mcp-download-title':'core-download-title';
import { Download, Code, Terminal } from '@lucide/astro';
const install='npm install --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund ./'+filename+'.tgz';
---
<div class:list={['distribution-download',isMcp?'mcp-download':'core-download']} aria-labelledby={titleId}>
 <div class="download-heading"><div><h3 id={titleId}>{isMcp?'Connect a local MCP host.':'Use the engine in your own tool.'}</h3></div><span class="small">v{distribution.version} · MIT · Node 22</span></div>
 <p>{isMcp?'Install the existing stdio server without copying a Composer checkout. It includes the same core source and adds two pinned transport dependencies: the official MCP SDK and Zod.':'Download the same library and command used by Composer. Keep your own interface, workspace and credentials. The archive has no runtime package dependencies or install scripts.'}</p>
 <div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href={distribution.url} download={filename+'.tgz'}><Download size={18} aria-hidden="true"/>{isMcp?'Download Get Ready MCP':'Download Get Ready'}</a><a href={'/downloads/'+filename+'.json'} download={filename+'.json'}>Source & checksum manifest</a></div>
 <p class="small">{Math.ceil(distribution.bytes/1024)} KB · {distribution.developmentSnapshot?'Development snapshot':'Release source'} <a href={`https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/tree/${distribution.source}/${isMcp?'lib/get-ready-mcp':'lib/get-ready'}`}><code>{distribution.source.slice(0,12)}</code></a>. Review the source and bundled README before running it. The manifest checks a matching download; it is not an independent signature.</p>
 {isMcp?<>
 <details><summary><Terminal size={17} aria-hidden="true"/>Install and select a local host</summary><p>Check the SHA-256 and review the bundled source. In a local integration folder chosen for this purpose, use Node 22 and install with lifecycle scripts disabled. This needs an approved npm registry for dependencies, or an already populated cache.</p><pre><code>{install}{'\n'}/absolute/integration/node_modules/.bin/get-ready-mcp --workspace /absolute/authorised/project</code></pre><p>Keep the generated dependency lockfile. Add the absolute executable and workspace to your host’s supported stdio configuration; the second line is a server launch, not a report command. No host configuration or account is changed by installation. Credentials stay in the host’s approved runtime handling, outside launch arguments.</p><p class="small">SHA-256 <code class="checksum">{distribution.sha256}</code></p></details>
 <details><summary><Code size={17} aria-hidden="true"/>Host scope and available tools</summary><p>The host fixes the workspace and any known account/reference before connecting. <code>get_ready_check</code> rechecks through the shared doctor and saves private progress; <code>get_ready_inspect</code> reads this connection’s last result. Tool calls keep the host’s selected scope. Checks cannot install, sign in or deploy.</p><p>The host can separately enable read-only domain diagnosis and project guidance inspection. To fetch the maintained Hello starter, an authorised host can select <code>--reference-destination /absolute/authorised/new-hello</code>. This enables <code>get_ready_clone_reference</code> for that destination only. Existing or partial files stay intact; no dependencies, project code, ownership or deployment run. The connection keeps its original workspace. Select the acquired checkout and any prepared tools explicitly on a new connection. Follow the bundled README for exact flags, disconnection and recovery. Missing targets remain incomplete. A protocol connection does not verify a customer environment or a particular agent’s capabilities.</p></details>
 <details><summary><Terminal size={17} aria-hidden="true"/>Optional tool preparation</summary><p>A host authorised to prepare missing local tools can add <code>--allow-workspace-tool-setup</code> to its launch. This enables the separate <code>get_ready_prepare_tools</code> operation for the selected workspace. It stays off by default.</p><p>The supported path is active Codex on an Apple Silicon Mac with Node 22 and Git. It verifies existing tools or prepares a pinned GitHub CLI/pnpm pair in the workspace’s private folder. Select the repository root or a folder outside Git; existing and partial copies stay intact. Global tools, profiles, sign-in, DNS and deployment are untouched.</p><p>The result reports what preparation completed alongside readiness. A working tool pair does not verify account access or an online app. Ordinary checks reuse a successfully prepared pair on this connection and never retry setup. After reconnecting, explicitly select the prepared folder with <code>--tools-workspace /absolute/authorised/project</code>, or request a separate preparation check. Inspect retained work before retrying a cancelled or failed operation.</p></details>
 </>:<>
 <details><summary><Terminal size={17} aria-hidden="true"/>Install the downloaded archive</summary><p>Check the archive’s SHA-256 against the manifest, then install it in the local project you chose for this integration.</p><pre><code>{install}{'\n'}./node_modules/.bin/get-ready --help{'\n'}./node_modules/.bin/get-ready --workspace /absolute/authorised/workspace --report</code></pre><p>The last command checks that workspace and saves private progress there. An incomplete setup returns a report with the next action. Setup operations require a separate explicit request. This archive is served here; it has not been published to the npm registry.</p><p class="small">SHA-256 <code class="checksum">{distribution.sha256}</code></p></details>
 <details><summary><Code size={17} aria-hidden="true"/>Library, API and MCP</summary><p>Import <code>runLocalReadiness</code>, <code>referenceTarget</code> and <code>readinessReport</code> from <code>@loftwah/get-ready</code>. The bundled README has a working integration example. Session and adapter exports use the same engine; report and address-preference modules have separate exports.</p><p>The optional <a href="#local-get-ready-mcp">local MCP adapter</a> has its own archive with SDK dependencies. Those dependencies are excluded from this core archive. A hosted API is still unexposed.</p></details>
 </>}

</div>
<style>
.distribution-download{border-block:1px solid var(--color-border);padding:28px 0;margin:28px 0}.download-heading{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;align-items:baseline}.download-heading h3{font-size:1.5rem;margin:8px 0 16px}.distribution-download details{margin-top:16px}.distribution-download summary{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;cursor:pointer;padding:10px 0;min-height:44px;font-weight:500}.distribution-download summary::before{content:'+';font:20px/1 system-ui}.distribution-download details[open] summary::before{content:'−'}.distribution-download summary svg{flex-shrink:0}.checksum{overflow-wrap:anywhere;word-break:break-all}.distribution-download pre{max-width:100%;overflow:auto;font-size:13px;padding:18px;background:var(--color-surface);border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:6px}.distribution-download p{max-width:76ch}@media(max-width:600px){.download-heading{flex-direction:column;gap:0}.download-heading h3{margin-bottom:10px}.download-heading>.small{margin-bottom:12px}.distribution-download{padding:24px 0}}
</style>
